#{[17592186344741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186342280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344741 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e69cb9d-fd4d-53e2-a61b-5d23ebea3ede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345182 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345182 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345182 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345182 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345182 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7615d87-ff46-5839-a075-3229075fb3df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345182 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ 1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366274 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67a843ac-f40c-59da-8377-455b0890a108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366274 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(5).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(5)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(5) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x}+1 & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366275 #edb/ref 107